What does one do when arriving in the dungeon to find an altar next to
the up staircase, followed by a wand of wishing in the next room over?
One hopes to ascend, of course! :-)

After a long string of dead vegan tourists, I decided to give up and
play a gnomish healer. I'd ascended offline with a human healer
before, but I don't think it was in this version (probably v3.2).
First NAO attempt failed; this gnome was the charmed one. I still
wanted to try for strict vegan conduct, but screwed up early with a
brown pudding, so settled for vegetarian. I spent one of those early
wand wishes on a ring of slow digestion, which would certainly not
have been my choice had I not been playing for conducts (I also held
to my usual no-polyself and no wishing for artifacts). Also got gray
dragon scale, figuring I could switch to silver later if I got a
cloak, and amulet of reflection. But I ended up going with GDSM + robe
+ amulet of reflection for the whole game. (Yay, survivor!)

The dungeon was littered with altars, including three co-aligned
temples. Unfortunately I never found Ludios, so wasn't very efficient
in getting protection. I spontaneously decided to try pudding farming
for the first time when encountering a black pudding near an altar. It
was rather boring and I'm not sure it was productive enough for me to
want to try again. But I did get some useful items, polypile fodder,
and both telepathy and stealth from Hermes, the former being most
welcome as being veggie I couldn't get telepathy the easy way, by
eating floating eyes.

Even before the pudding sacfest I had Mjollnir, Vorpal Blade, Frost
and Fire Brand, eventually jettisoning all but Frosty to save weight.
I switched to the Staff of Aesculapius after the quest (was lazy and
used a wand of death on the Cyclops, as I'd found several already).
Farming granted me Magicbane, which I kept in the alternate weaponslot
for use in the ascension run. By the time I got the castle wand, I
didn't really need it; I'd finished up the level-one wand by wishing
for magic markers, and found or polypiled for the rest of the armor
needed (gauntlets of dexterity, helm of brilliance, speed boots).
Sokoban had the bag of holding - good thing because I really hate
Soko, and would have cursed out loud had the prize been the amulet I
was already wearing.

After the boredom of farming, I wanted to get through Gehennom as fast
as possible. Abandoning my usual tactic of walking every corridor in
hopes of finding more useful items, I went down as soon as I'd found
both staircases and chopped a path between them. I also paid off
Asmodeus and Baalzebub for the first time; I couldn't believe how
little money they asked for (I actually gave them a bit more, $666,
for fun :-D ). Something summoned Yeenoghu; charm monster turned him
peaceful, but always underfoot; I left him in the castle. Juiblex and
Vlad were easy as always; Orcus not much tougher, though I was more
annoyed that he didn't leave me any charges on his wand of death.
Killed Rodney seven times total; took awhile in the Sanctum, as I
forgot I had a cockatrice corpse, and wands of death missed him at
first most of the other times. But he never managed to steal the
Amulet nor the Staff. Got the annoying mysterious force so many times
that I quickly ran out of teleport wands and scrolls. Eventually made
it to level one and managed to drop enough to become unburdened for
the endgame. Planes were very easy, and I actually got a guardian
angel for the first time on Astral as I had taken off my ring of
conflict for once. (I put it back on soon though. :-P ) Since I was
unburdened, another thing I wanted to try was jumping, as I'd heard
that was a useful Astral strategy, but I kept bumping into hostile
Angels; might as well have left the speed boots on. After taking out
Famine a couple of times, I headed for the central altar which was
luckily the right one, and I was done.

